# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How to check this rather than believe it
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. Read the request off the chain. It is a public text record:
#
#      node scripts/agent.mjs show
#
#    It was written by tx 0x14796928813fd4b495c6a3442c0207d719ab98e4154fb205d1ee7601d7ea485c,
#    signed by the agent's own key, under an ENS role that permits that one
#    record on that one name and nothing else.
#
# 2. Hash it. `show` prints the hash; `bun test` asserts it independently.
#    It is 0xb74ac56696e0e84612546123e2ec0a495a5b071be625b10141f2af4f59ce5336.
#
# 3. Compare with the hash in the verdict above. They agree, so the enclave
#    judged this request — not a different one, and not a modified one.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What crossed back out of the enclave, and what did not
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Out:  the request id, the verdict, a coarse reason, and the hash of a record
#       that was already public.
#
# Not out:  who the guardians are, how many approved, what the quorum is, how
#       far the request was from its threshold, and the API credential. The
#       distance to a threshold is itself useful to an attacker — which is why
#       the reason is `quorum_not_met` rather than `1_of_2`.
#
# Honest scope: CRE protects data *during execution*, not source code. This
# workflow is public, as it should be for an open-source project. The claim is
# narrow and true — the secret never passes a node an operator can inspect. It
# is not a claim that the release logic is hidden.
#
# Also honest: the guardian approvals in fixtures/release-request.json are
# fabricated stand-ins. Guardians are not built yet. The request they surround
# is real and on chain; the people are not, and the fixture says so in its
# first field.
